        <!-- How it works -->
        <div class="mt-12 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">How This Resizer Works</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
                When you pick a file, your browser reads it into memory and decodes it as a picture. The tool then draws that picture onto an invisible canvas at the new width and height, and asks the browser to encode the canvas as a fresh PNG, JPEG or WebP file. The File Size line is the size of that fresh file, recalculated every time you move a slider, so what you see is exactly what the Download button saves. No part of this leaves your computer, which is why the tool keeps working if you switch the internet off after the page has loaded.
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li><strong>Percentage</strong> scales both sides by the same factor, from 10 to 200 percent.</li>
                <li><strong>Custom Dimensions</strong> sets exact pixel values; with Maintain aspect ratio ticked, typing one side fills in the other.</li>
                <li><strong>Preset Sizes</strong> jumps to common screen sizes such as 1920 by 1080 or 512 by 512.</li>
                <li><strong>Output Format</strong> chooses the encoder. The quality slider applies to JPEG and WebP and is hidden for PNG, which is always lossless.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Two side effects of re-encoding are worth knowing. The new file carries none of the original's metadata, so GPS coordinates and camera details from a phone photo are gone. And an animated GIF loses its animation, because only the first frame is drawn.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Resizing vs compressing -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Resizing Dimensions vs Compressing Quality</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
                Two different things shrink an image file. Resizing reduces the number of pixels: halving the width and the height leaves a quarter of the pixels, and the file size falls by roughly the same proportion. Compressing keeps every pixel but stores the colours less precisely, which is what the quality slider controls for JPEG and WebP. At 90 percent the loss is invisible; at 70 to 80 percent the file is far smaller and the photo still looks fine on a screen; below 50 percent blocky patches appear around edges and in smooth areas such as skin and sky.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Dimensions matter more than quality. A 12-megapixel phone photo is around 4000 by 3000 pixels, and no quality setting will bring it near 50 KB. Once the same photo is 413 by 531 pixels, it lands under 50 KB at a quality most people cannot tell from the original. Resize first, then compress.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Formats -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">JPEG, PNG or WebP?</h2>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-600">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Format</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Best for</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Transparency</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Notes</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-500">
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">JPEG (.jpg)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Photographs, scanned photos, anything with a size limit</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">No</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Lossy. The quality slider trades sharpness for size. Accepted by every portal.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">PNG</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Screenshots, logos, text, scanned signatures</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Lossless, so edges stay crisp. File size depends only on pixel count and detail; the only way to shrink it is fewer pixels.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">WebP</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Websites, sharing, storage</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">Usually 25 to 35 percent smaller than JPEG at similar quality. All current browsers open it, but many government forms accept only JPG or PNG.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                One trap when converting: JPEG has no transparency, so a PNG logo or signature with a transparent background comes out with a black background after conversion. Keep PNG for those, or flatten the image onto white first.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Target size for forms -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Hitting a Target Size for Application Forms</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
                Exam portals, job applications and university admissions reject uploads that miss their photo and signature rules, and every portal publishes its own figures in the notification or the upload page. Most ask for a photograph of a few tens of kilobytes at a size close to a printed passport photo, a signature of around 10 to 30 KB in a wide, short frame, and scanned documents in the range of 100 KB to 1 MB. The numbers differ between portals and change between years, so copy them from the page you are uploading to, and then:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4">
                <li>Choose Custom Dimensions, untick Maintain aspect ratio if the portal gives both a width and a height, and type the pixel values it asks for. If it gives centimetres, use the DPI table below.</li>
                <li>Pick the format the portal accepts. JPEG is the safe choice for photographs; use PNG only if the form asks for it.</li>
                <li>Lower the quality slider until the File Size line is inside the allowed range. Stay above the minimum too: some portals reject files that are too small.</li>
                <li>Download, then open the file once to confirm it is not blurred and that the face or signature fills the frame.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Signatures are easiest scanned or photographed on plain white paper in black ink, cropped tight to the ink before resizing. The article on <a href="/blog/image-dimensions-indian-job-applications/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 underline">image dimensions for Indian job applications</a> walks through the photo and signature process step by step, and <a href="/blog/how-to-compress-and-resize-images-without-losing-quality/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 underline">how to compress and resize images without losing quality</a> covers the trade-offs in more depth.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- DPI -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Pixels, Centimetres and DPI</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
                A digital image has no physical size, only a number of pixels. DPI (dots per inch) is a print setting that says how densely those pixels should be laid on paper, so a 3.5 by 4.5 cm photo printed at 300 DPI needs 413 by 531 pixels (divide the centimetres by 2.54 and multiply by 300). Portals that quote a size in centimetres or a DPI value are really asking for a pixel count, and this tool does not write a DPI value into the file at all.
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-600">
                        <tr>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Printed size</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Pixels at 200 DPI</th>
                            <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left border dark:border-gray-500 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">Pixels at 300 DPI</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-500">
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">3.5 x 4.5 cm (common Indian passport size)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">276 x 354</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">413 x 531</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">2 x 2 inch (51 x 51 mm)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">400 x 400</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">600 x 600</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500 font-semibold">A4 sheet (21 x 29.7 cm)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">1654 x 2339</td><td class="px-4 py-3 border dark:border-gray-500">2480 x 3508</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Privacy -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-4">Metadata and Privacy</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                Photos from a phone carry EXIF metadata: the exact time, the camera model, the settings, and often the GPS coordinates of where the picture was taken. Uploading such a file to a form or a marketplace listing hands all of that over. Because this tool builds a new file from the pixels alone, the download contains none of the original metadata. Resizing a photo here before sharing it is therefore also a quick way to strip location data. The one thing to check is orientation: browsers apply the phone's rotation tag while decoding, so a portrait photo stays upright, but if a very old file appears sideways, rotate it in your gallery app before uploading.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="mt-8 bg-white dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-lg shadow-lg border-2 border-gray-900 dark:border-gray-700 p-8 transition-colors duration-200">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-white mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <div class="space-y-4">
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        How do I reduce a photo to 20 KB or 50 KB for an online form?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Set the pixel dimensions the form asks for first, using Custom Dimensions. Then choose JPEG as the output format and lower the quality slider until the File Size line drops under your target. Dimensions do most of the work: a 4000 pixel phone photo will not reach 50 KB at any quality, but a 413 by 531 pixel photo will at around 80 percent.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Does resizing happen on my device or on a server?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">On your device. The image is read by your browser, drawn onto an invisible canvas at the new size and re-encoded there. Nothing is uploaded, so the tool also works with the internet switched off once the page has loaded.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        What is the difference between resizing and compressing?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">Resizing changes the number of pixels, which changes both the picture's dimensions and its file size. Compressing keeps the pixels and reduces the file size by storing them less precisely, which is what the quality slider does for JPEG and WebP. Most forms need both: the right pixel size and a file under a size limit.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Which format should I choose: JPEG, PNG or WebP?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">JPEG for photographs and anything with a size limit, PNG for screenshots, logos and scanned signatures where sharp edges and transparency matter, and WebP when the destination accepts it and you want the smallest file. Government portals usually accept only JPG or PNG, so check the form before choosing WebP.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Why did the background turn black after converting to JPEG?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">JPEG cannot store transparency. When a PNG with a transparent background is converted, the transparent area is filled with black by the browser. Keep PNG or WebP for images with transparency, or place the image on a white background before converting.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Does the resized image keep the location data from my phone?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">No. Phone cameras store EXIF metadata such as GPS coordinates, the date and the device model inside the file. The canvas re-encoding used here writes a fresh file with none of that metadata, so the download is clean.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        What pixel size is a 3.5 by 4.5 cm photo at 300 DPI?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">413 by 531 pixels. Divide each centimetre figure by 2.54 to get inches, then multiply by the DPI. At 200 DPI the same photo is 276 by 354 pixels. This tool does not write a DPI value into the file; portals that ask for one are really asking for a pixel size.</p>
                </details>
                <details class="group bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-600 rounded-lg p-4 cursor-pointer">
                    <summary class="font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-white flex justify-between items-center">
                        Does it work with animated GIFs or PDF files?
                        <i class="fas fa-chevron-down text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"></i>
                    </summary>
                    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-sm">A GIF is accepted, but only its first frame is resized and the animation is lost. PDFs are not images and cannot be opened here; export the page as a JPG or PNG first, then resize that.</p>
                </details>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</main>

{% block scripts %}
<script>
    let originalImage = null;
    let canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
    let ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

    function handleFileSelect(event) {
        const file = event.target.files[0];
        if (!file) return;

        const reader = new FileReader();
        reader.onload = function (e) {
            const img = new Image();
            img.onload = function () {
                originalImage = img;
                document.getElementById('customWidth').value = img.width;
                document.getElementById('customHeight').value = img.height;
                document.getElementById('originalSize').textContent = img.width + ' × ' + img.height + ' px';

                // Show editor, hide upload
                document.getElementById('uploadSection').classList.add('hidden');
                document.getElementById('editorSection').classList.remove('hidden');

                // Initial render
                resizeImage();
            };
            img.src = e.target.result;
        };
        reader.readAsDataURL(file);
    }

    function updateResizeMode() {
        const mode = document.getElementById('resizeMode').value;

        document.getElementById('percentageMode').classList.add('hidden');
        document.getElementById('dimensionsMode').classList.add('hidden');
        document.getElementById('presetMode').classList.add('hidden');

        if (mode === 'percentage') {
            document.getElementById('percentageMode').classList.remove('hidden');
        } else if (mode === 'dimensions') {
            document.getElementById('dimensionsMode').classList.remove('hidden');
        } else if (mode === 'preset') {
            document.getElementById('presetMode').classList.remove('hidden');
        }

        resizeImage();
    }

    function updatePercentage() {
        const value = document.getElementById('percentage').value;
        document.getElementById('percentageValue').textContent = value;
        resizeImage();
    }

    function updateQuality() {
        const value = document.getElementById('quality').value;
        document.getElementById('qualityValue').textContent = value;
        resizeImage();
    }

    function updateCustomDimensions(changedDimension) {
        if (!originalImage) return;

        const maintainRatio = document.getElementById('maintainRatio').checked;
        if (!maintainRatio) {
            resizeImage();
            return;
        }

        const widthVal = document.getElementById('customWidth').value;
        const heightVal = document.getElementById('customHeight').value;
        const ratio = originalImage.width / originalImage.height;

        if (changedDimension === 'width') {
            const width = parseInt(widthVal);
            if (!isNaN(width) && width > 0) {
                document.getElementById('customHeight').value = Math.round(width / ratio);
            }
        } else {
            const height = parseInt(heightVal);
            if (!isNaN(height) && height > 0) {
                document.getElementById('customWidth').value = Math.round(height * ratio);
            }
        }

        resizeImage();
    }

    function applyPreset() {
        const preset = document.getElementById('presetSize').value;
        if (!preset) return;

        const [width, height] = preset.split(',').map(Number);
        document.getElementById('customWidth').value = width;
        document.getElementById('customHeight').value = height;
        resizeImage();
    }

    function resizeImage() {
        if (!originalImage) return;

        const mode = document.getElementById('resizeMode').value;
        let newWidth, newHeight;

        if (mode === 'percentage') {
            const percentage = parseInt(document.getElementById('percentage').value) / 100;
            newWidth = Math.round(originalImage.width * percentage);
            newHeight = Math.round(originalImage.height * percentage);
        } else {
            newWidth = parseInt(document.getElementById('customWidth').value) || originalImage.width;
            newHeight = parseInt(document.getElementById('customHeight').value) || originalImage.height;
        }

        // Resize canvas
        canvas.width = newWidth;
        canvas.height = newHeight;

        // Draw image
        ctx.drawImage(originalImage, 0, 0, newWidth, newHeight);

        // Update info
        document.getElementById('newSize').textContent = newWidth + ' × ' + newHeight + ' px';

        // Estimate file size
        const format = document.getElementById('outputFormat').value;
        const quality = parseInt(document.getElementById('quality').value) / 100;
        canvas.toBlob((blob) => {
            if (blob) {
                const sizeMB = (blob.size / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(2);
                const sizeKB = (blob.size / 1024).toFixed(2);
                document.getElementById('fileSize').textContent = sizeMB > 1 ? sizeMB + ' MB' : sizeKB + ' KB';
            }
        }, format, quality);
    }

    function downloadImage() {
        // GA4 Event Tracking
        trackCalculatorUsage('Image Resizer', 'download');

        const format = document.getElementById('outputFormat').value;
        const quality = parseInt(document.getElementById('quality').value) / 100;

        canvas.toBlob((blob) => {
            const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
            const a = document.createElement('a');
            a.href = url;

            // Set filename with proper extension
            let extension = 'png';
            if (format === 'image/jpeg') extension = 'jpg';
            else if (format === 'image/webp') extension = 'webp';

            a.download = 'resized-image.' + extension;
            a.click();
            URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
        }, format, quality);
    }

    function resetTool() {
        originalImage = null;
        document.getElementById('uploadSection').classList.remove('hidden');
        document.getElementById('editorSection').classList.add('hidden');
        document.getElementById('fileInput').value = '';
    }

    // Update quality control visibility based on format
    document.getElementById('outputFormat').addEventListener('change', function () {
        const format = this.value;
        const qualityControl = document.getElementById('qualityControl');

        if (format === 'image/png') {
            qualityControl.classList.add('hidden');
        } else {
            qualityControl.classList.remove('hidden');
        }
        resizeImage();
    });

    // Drag and drop support
    const uploadSection = document.getElementById('uploadSection');
    uploadSection.addEventListener('dragover', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopPropagation();
    });

    uploadSection.addEventListener('drop', (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.stopPropagation();
        const files = e.dataTransfer.files;
        if (files.length > 0) {
            document.getElementById('fileInput').files = files;
            handleFileSelect({ target: { files: files } });
        }
    });
</script>
{% endblock %}
